    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21809",
      "problem": "Let $F(x)=x^2+10x$ for $x\\in[0,6),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-6,6).$ What is the value of $f(-1)?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,6),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14227643/assets/3c8c74113929c2026d4ac988fa1d0caaef29677ae17cf7512477114c3f793898.svg)\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+10(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-10x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+10(-1)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
